#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084117} :activity/id #uuid "35a2312a-8238-5672-8715-5b99766aa324" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048254 :topic/id #uuid "8c8c4388-1538-58d1-b482-6285767c2635" :topic/math-academy-id 703} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084448 :tutorial/content "A **permutation** is a selection of objects in which order matters. Two selections of the same items are counted as different selections if they are in a different order.\n\nFor example, suppose that we have $5$ books, but our bookshelf can only hold $3.$ We want to place $3$ books on the shelf and the rest will stay in the drawer. \n\nHow many permutations are there? In other words, how many ways can we place $3$ books on the shelf (in a particular order)?\n\nLet's think about all the choices we have as we fill up the bookshelf:\n\n- We can place any of our $5$ books in the first slot of the bookshelf.\n\n- Now that we've placed one book on the bookshelf, we have $4$ books remaining. We can place any of these $4$ remaining books in the second slot.\n\n- Now that we've placed two books on the bookshelf, we have $3$ books remaining. We can place any of these $3$ remaining books in the third slot.\n\nThe number of choices we have for each slot is shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{5 choices} & \\text{4 choices} & \\text{3 choices}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of arrangements, we can use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n5\\times4\\times3 = 60.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e27ded9-9ee6-527a-9381-e7f6d0396030" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3682 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f34cb92-fb54-5dc9-8f17-9675bc62c341" :step/math-academy-id 20569 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084246}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083021 :question/id #uuid "258b1f90-1c19-5a19-b796-a7c1c894bf17" :question/math-academy-id "e-5673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050268}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394620}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"} :step/id #uuid "f972487c-b2d6-5f63-935f-fb91b47f99b7" :step/math-academy-id 20570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083222 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083022 :question/id #uuid "5431e96c-87d1-517b-a565-9621a0b67913" :question/math-academy-id "e-5674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392763}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"} :step/id #uuid "39129bcf-9379-546f-a458-1226fc31f8fc" :step/math-academy-id 20571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083023 :question/id #uuid "c72ffaba-7a68-5070-920d-fe241be5c797" :question/math-academy-id "e-5675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394641}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"} :step/id #uuid "a131104f-d99f-5253-bcb9-6403049716e4" :step/math-academy-id 20573 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084471 :tutorial/content "In general, the number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n$$\n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books and $r=3$ slots in the bookshelf. Using the formula, the total number of permutations is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{(5-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2!}{2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 4\\times 3\\times \\cancel{2!}}{\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =5\\times 4\\times 3 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** We can write ${}_nP_r$ in a number of ways:\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = {}^nP_r = P(n,r)\n$$\n\nAll of these mean the same thing." :tutorial/id #uuid "7e83bfaa-0308-5b07-a516-02b67001ac8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4477 :tutorial/title "The General Formula for Permutations"} :step/id #uuid "5daedd62-435e-56b3-aa28-cfa9a1b10d0f" :step/math-academy-id 27561 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083111 :question/id #uuid "a18e8e9a-da60-5a42-840a-60f83137c6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394634}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation"} :step/id #uuid "ccdc7daa-24d6-512d-8379-a36e4ffbe8a3" :step/math-academy-id 27676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084120}}] :activity/title "Permutations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083409}]}
